Subject: [PATCH 11/11] set-defaults runs in the wrong list, and a fault recovery
 that never reached the interpreter

Two defects that a green suite could not see, both inherited by arch/amd64 from
arch/x86 and both present upstream.

1. arch/{x86,amd64,ia64}/init.fs register `set-defaults` as a SYSTEM-initializer.
   arch_init -- which calls nvconf_init() -- is registered from C as a PREPOST
   one, and PREPOST runs first, so every boot read the NVRAM store, parsed it,
   applied it to /options, and then overwrote it with the compile-time defaults.
   ppc/qemu, sparc32 and sparc64 all say PREPOST-initializer. Say it here too.

2. arch/amd64/exception.c recovered by zeroing the Forth stacks, aiming PC at
   outer-interpreter, and returning through a do-nothing function so the C stack
   would unwind into enterforth's loop. That loop is `while (rstackcnt > tmp)`
   with tmp captured at entry, so rstackcnt = 0 makes it false for every frame:
   measured, the frame it returned into had tmp=8 and exited without calling
   next() once, and four nested frames unwound the same way. The chosen PC was
   never executed -- the machine printed one " ok" and stopped listening.

   Abandon the faulted stack instead: iretq onto _estack and re-enter the outer
   interpreter from scratch. A faulted C stack has no invariants left, and
   recovering through it makes recovery depend on where the fault landed.

   (The `+ sizeof(ucell)` this file carried was a separate error in the other
   direction: kernel/forth.c:686 needs it because next_dbg() has already
   advanced PC; next() advances it itself, so arch/x86's spelling was right.)

diff --git a/arch/amd64/init.fs b/arch/amd64/init.fs
index fda3acd..ececba9 100644
--- a/arch/amd64/init.fs
+++ b/arch/amd64/init.fs
@@ -51,9 +51,22 @@ finish-device
   then
 ;
  
+\ WHEN this runs is the whole of it. set-defaults overwrites EVERY config
+\ variable with its compile-time default, so it has to run BEFORE the NVRAM
+\ store is read, never after. arch_init -- which calls nvconf_init() -- is
+\ registered from C as a PREPOST-initializer, and list-add APPENDS, so a
+\ PREPOST entry compiled into the dictionary here is reached first and the
+\ store's values land on top of the defaults, which is the intent.
+\
+\ As a SYSTEM-initializer (what x86, amd64 and ia64 all shipped) it runs in
+\ the NEXT list instead, i.e. AFTER nvconf_init has loaded the store -- so a
+\ perfectly good store was read, parsed, applied to /options, and then thrown
+\ away every boot. ppc/qemu, sparc32 and sparc64 -- the three arches anyone
+\ actually boots -- all say PREPOST-initializer here. The never-finished
+\ three copied the line into the wrong list.
 :noname
   set-defaults
-; SYSTEM-initializer
+; PREPOST-initializer
 
 \ preopen device nodes (and store the ihandles under /chosen)
 :noname
diff --git a/arch/x86/init.fs b/arch/x86/init.fs
index eef72e9..2c86959 100644
--- a/arch/x86/init.fs
+++ b/arch/x86/init.fs
@@ -52,9 +52,22 @@ finish-device
   device-end
 ;
  
+\ WHEN this runs is the whole of it. set-defaults overwrites EVERY config
+\ variable with its compile-time default, so it has to run BEFORE the NVRAM
+\ store is read, never after. arch_init -- which calls nvconf_init() -- is
+\ registered from C as a PREPOST-initializer, and list-add APPENDS, so a
+\ PREPOST entry compiled into the dictionary here is reached first and the
+\ store's values land on top of the defaults, which is the intent.
+\
+\ As a SYSTEM-initializer (what x86, amd64 and ia64 all shipped) it runs in
+\ the NEXT list instead, i.e. AFTER nvconf_init has loaded the store -- so a
+\ perfectly good store was read, parsed, applied to /options, and then thrown
+\ away every boot. ppc/qemu, sparc32 and sparc64 -- the three arches anyone
+\ actually boots -- all say PREPOST-initializer here. The never-finished
+\ three copied the line into the wrong list.
 :noname
   set-defaults
-; SYSTEM-initializer
+; PREPOST-initializer
 
 \ preopen device nodes (and store the ihandles under /chosen)
 :noname
--- a/arch/amd64/exception.c
+++ b/arch/amd64/exception.c
@@ -61,15 +61,46 @@
 	"machine check exception",
 };
 
-void do_nothing(void);
-void do_nothing(void)
+/* The firmware's own stack top, from arch/amd64/ldscript. Recovery abandons
+ * the faulted stack and starts again on this one -- see restart_interpreter. */
+extern char _estack[];
+
+/* Set between the fault and the moment the interpreter is actually re-entered.
+ * A second fault inside that window would recurse forever on a fresh stack
+ * each time, printing the same dump until the console scrolls the original
+ * off; halting once is the honest answer. */
+static volatile int recovering;
+
+static void halt_forever(void)
+{
+	for (;;)
+		asm volatile ("cli; hlt");
+}
+
+/* Never returns. Entered by iretq on a FRESH STACK, with the faulted one
+ * abandoned entirely -- see the long note in amd64_exception. */
+static void restart_interpreter(void)
 {
+	recovering = 0;
+
+	rstackcnt = 0;
+	dstackcnt = 0;
+
+	PC = (ucell)findword("outer-interpreter");
+	enterforth((xt_t)PC);
+
+	/* The outer interpreter is a loop; reaching here means it fell out,
+	 * which recovery cannot paper over. Say so rather than returning into
+	 * a stack that no longer exists. */
+	printk("\nthe outer interpreter returned after a fault -- halting.\n");
+	halt_forever();
 }
 
 void amd64_exception(struct eregs *info);
 void amd64_exception(struct eregs *info)
 {
 	uint64_t cr2 = 0;
+	uint64_t sp;
 
 	if (info->vector <= 18)
 		printk("\nUnexpected Exception: %s", exception_names[info->vector]);
@@ -110,25 +141,49 @@
 	 * so once is worth more than pretending a hundred times. */
 	if (!dict) {
 		printk("\nFault before the dictionary was loaded -- halting.\n");
-		for (;;)
-			asm volatile ("cli; hlt");
+		halt_forever();
 	}
 
-	/* Recover rather than halt, exactly as arch/x86 does: empty the Forth
-	 * stacks, aim the interpreter at its outer loop, and return through a
-	 * function that does nothing. iretq then resumes there instead of at
-	 * the instruction that faulted. */
-	rstackcnt = 0;
-	dstackcnt = 0;
+	if (recovering) {
+		printk("\nA second fault arrived before the interpreter restarted"
+		       " -- halting.\n");
+		halt_forever();
+	}
+	recovering = 1;
 
-	/* + sizeof(ucell), which arch/x86's copy of this line OMITS.
-	 * findword() returns the word's HEADER; the interpreter has to be
-	 * entered at its BODY, one cell along. kernel/forth.c:686 -- the
-	 * engine's own "start a subordinate interpreter" path -- gets this
-	 * right: `PC = findword("outer-interpreter") + sizeof(ucell)`.
-	 * Without the offset the machine survives the fault, prints " ok",
-	 * and then accepts no further input: alive, and not listening. */
-	PC = findword("outer-interpreter") + sizeof(ucell);
+	/* THE FAULTED C STACK CANNOT BE RECOVERED THROUGH, and arch/x86's
+	 * handler -- which this was copied from -- has always tried to.
+	 *
+	 * Its shape is: zero the Forth stacks, point PC at the outer
+	 * interpreter, and resume at a do-nothing function so the C stack
+	 * unwinds back into enterforth's loop, which then runs the new PC.
+	 * The last step is the one that never happens. enterforth's loop is
+	 *
+	 *     tmp = rstackcnt; ... while (rstackcnt > tmp && ...) next();
+	 *
+	 * so setting rstackcnt = 0 makes that condition false for EVERY
+	 * enterforth frame on the stack. Measured here on 2026-08-23 with a
+	 * page fault at the prompt: the frame we returned into had tmp = 8,
+	 * exited without calling next() even once, and four more nested frames
+	 * unwound the same way. The carefully-chosen PC was never executed.
+	 * The machine survived, printed one " ok", and then accepted no input
+	 * -- alive and not listening, which is exactly the reported symptom.
+	 *
+	 * (The `+ sizeof(ucell)` this file used to carry was a separate error
+	 * in the other direction. kernel/forth.c:686 needs that offset because
+	 * next_dbg() has ALREADY advanced PC before calling the debugger;
+	 * next() advances it itself, so a handler that returns into the loop
+	 * must NOT pre-add it. Both spellings wedge identically -- measured --
+	 * because the loop is never reached either way.)
+	 *
+	 * So: do not return into it. iretq onto the firmware's own stack and
+	 * enter the outer interpreter from scratch. Nothing on the faulted
+	 * stack is worth keeping -- its invariants died with the fault -- and
+	 * abandoning it is what makes the recovery independent of where the
+	 * fault happened to land. */
+	sp = ((uint64_t)(uintptr_t)_estack) & ~0xFULL;
+	sp -= 8;		/* as a CALL would leave it: rsp % 16 == 8 */
 
-	info->rip = (uint64_t)&do_nothing;
+	info->rsp = sp;
+	info->rip = (uint64_t)(uintptr_t)&restart_interpreter;
 }
